ing  so it could be that it's just such a  slow and gentle process  that  ecosystems and evolution and everything  can sort of keep up with the change what  we might be concerned about is increased  particle radiation we know that the  magnetic field of the earth protects us  from highly charged particles it might  be that more cosmic rays are able to  reach the ground with a weak magnetic  field but the atmosphere itself does a  pretty good job of protecting us as well  so it might be that we just get  interesting effects like aurora seen  over you know the galapagos islands or  something like that it might not be as  catastrophic as you might fear so of  course the next question is  what are the magnetic fields like on  other planets so all of the gas giants  have strong magnetic fields jupiter  unsurprisingly has the biggest of them  all and you can actually hear the sound  of jupiter's magnetic field if you look  at the recordings from the juno  spacecraft which reached jupiter a few  years ago  recorded bow shock as the instrument  passed through jupiter's magnetic field  so no surprises there  mercury has a weak one venus and mars  don't seem to have any at all and the  relative strength is tied to  what's happening in the core mars has a  solid core we think no conduction no  rotation so that's not going to generate  a magnetic field that might go some way  to explaining why mars has lost the  atmosphere that it used to have the  magnetic field might have some sort of  confining action there but the really  interesting one is uranus of course  uranus is an interesting planet in its  own right because of course its axis of  rotation is tilted at 98 degrees to the  plane of the ecliptic which since we've  been talking about everything being  connected to rotation what does uranus's  magnetic field look like and it's a  complete mess  the axis of the magnetic field in uranus  is actually tilted at 60 degrees from  the axis of rotation and what that means  is the whole field is tumbling as the  planet rotates which means that the  magnetic field lines detach and then  reattach in really chaotic ways which  means that the  the planet actually sort of has a strobe  effect with its magnetic field that it  opens up and lets charge particles in  and then snaps closed again opens and  closes and opens closes presumably the  sun has a magnetic field it does it's  generated in a slightly different way  but of course it has a very large  magnetic field we know that it waxes and  wanes on a quite regular time scale of  11 years and that's tied into solar  activity and sunspot cycles you can see  the beautiful magnetic loops that are  created from that there's still a lot we  don't exactly know like there is like it  is still one of those sort of mystery  subjects there's lots we do know but it  seems like there's lots we don't know  yeah i think that's correct and when you  think about what's going into all of  this the very very complex interplay  between all of the different systems the  difficulty in understanding what's going  on below the surface of the earth 3000  kilometers below the surface of the  earth and making guesswork there plus  the fact that we've only been making  these measurements for a few hundred  years um yeah i think it's a very uh  complicated  field but one i'm sure that's going to  make lots of progress in in the near  future different from the it's not just  a scaled-up version of that  electromagnet it's what's called a  superconducting magnet  so  actually when i applied a current to  that elect that little mini  electromagnet with the orange wire  earlier on
 

